<main id="main" class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head">
    <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
    <p class="lede">Written for: a U.S. citizen, California resident, ages 19&ndash;64 (assumed), with $0 earned income &mdash; money comes from Kalshi trading contracts, rental property, and savings interest, and the person works as a self-directed property manager and full-time trader.</p>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="bottom-h">
    <h3 id="bottom-h">The bottom line</h3>
    <div class="panel accent">
      <ol>
        <li><strong>You do not need earned income to obtain health coverage in California.</strong> Both Medi-Cal and Covered California set eligibility on household MAGI, and MAGI already counts your rental, interest, and trading income &mdash; it does not require wages. (<a href="https://www.healthcare.gov/income-and-household-information/income/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Healthcare.gov income rules</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>Today, Medi-Cal has no work requirement at all.</strong> If your household MAGI is at or below 138% FPL ($22,025/year for one person in 2026), you can enroll in Medi-Cal with no premium, no asset test, and no work test &mdash; and keep it through December 31, 2026 on exactly those terms. (<a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/medi-cal/updates/medi-cal-changes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS Medi-Cal Changes</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>From January 1, 2027, a federal work and community engagement rule applies to Medi-Cal expansion adults &mdash; but it is an hours-or-income rule, not an earned-income rule.</strong> You can satisfy it with zero earned income by: monthly income of at least <strong>$580 from any source</strong> (per DHCS's member outreach letter); <strong>80 hours/month of unpaid community service</strong>; <strong>at least half-time school</strong>; a work program; any combination; or a qualifying exemption. (<a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/MEDIL-I26-25.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS MEDIL I26-25</a>; <a href="https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/medicaid-community-engagement-requirement-certain-individuals-interim-final-rule-comment-period-cms" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CMS fact sheet</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>If your MAGI is above 138% FPL, Covered California has no work requirement whatsoever.</strong> Federal premium tax credits cover households 100&ndash;400% FPL, California adds state subsidies up to 200% FPL for 2027, and Silver cost-sharing reductions help up to 250% FPL. Open enrollment runs November 1, 2026 &ndash; January 31, 2027. (<a href="https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/premium-tax-credit/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Healthcare.gov</a>; <a href="https://www.coveredca.com/press-releases/2026/07/21/covered-california-rates-and-plans-for-2027-california-continues-fight-for-health-insurance-affordability-and-access" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Covered California</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>Your income streams all count toward MAGI, and none of them is "earned income."</strong> Interest, capital gains (including Kalshi trading results), and net rental income are unearned &mdash; but they are all counted for Medi-Cal and premium tax credits, and (per DHCS) the $580 work-rule income standard counts income <em>from any source</em>. (<a href="https://healthcare.gov/income-and-household-information/income/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Healthcare.gov</a>; <a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/MEDIL-I26-25.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>California added protections.</strong> AB 2161 (Chapter 209, Statutes of 2026, signed September 14, 2026) requires counties to verify from existing data before demanding paperwork, to keep Medi-Cal active during the 30-day noncompliance response window, and to check every other basis of Medi-Cal eligibility before denying or terminating anyone. (<a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260AB2161" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AB 2161 bill text</a>)</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="doors-h">
    <h3 id="doors-h">Your decision paths</h3>
    <p>Which door applies depends on one number &mdash; your household MAGI for the coverage year (tax filer + spouse + tax dependents; see the <a href="income-treatment.html">Income &amp; MAGI</a> page). Note that trading losses, depreciation, and rental losses can lower MAGI, so the number can move year to year.</p>
    {{TABLE:options-summary.csv::Decision paths for a California resident with $0 earned income}}
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="law-h">
    <h3 id="law-h">The new law in plain English</h3>
    <div class="panel blue">
      <p><strong>What changed.</strong> The One Big Beautiful Bill Act (H.R. 1, Public Law 119-21, signed July 4, 2025) added a &ldquo;community engagement requirement&rdquo; to Medicaid via <a href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1/text" target="_blank" rel="noopener">section 71119</a> (new section 1902(xx) of the Social Security Act). CMS turned it into an operating rule on June 1, 2026 (<a href="https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/06/03/2026-11094/medicaid-program-community-engagement-requirement-for-certain-individuals" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CMS-2454-IFC</a>, 91 FR 33348, effective July 31, 2026).</p>
      <p><strong>Who it covers.</strong> Non-pregnant adults 19&ndash;64, not on Medicare, who get Medicaid through the ACA expansion group (in California, the MAGI &ldquo;New Adult Group&rdquo;). People with MAGI above Medi-Cal limits are not in this group at all &mdash; they use Covered California, which has no work requirement.</p>
      <p><strong>What it requires.</strong> Each month: 80 hours of work, community service, or a work program; or at least half-time school; or any combination; or monthly income of at least 80 &times; the federal minimum wage ($580 in 2026). New applicants must show compliance for at least one month before applying; existing members are checked at renewal (every six months under section 71107). Noncompliance triggers a notice with a 30-day cure period before coverage ends; reapplication is allowed at any time.</p>
      <p><strong>Exemptions.</strong> Parents/caregivers of a child 13 or younger (or of a disabled person), pregnancy and 12-month postpartum, disability, medical frailty and serious health conditions, American Indians/Alaska Natives, totally disabled veterans, former foster youth under 26, people meeting CalWORKs/TANF rules, CalFresh households subject to SNAP work rules, people in substance-use treatment, and inmates. States may add short-term hardship exceptions (hospitalization, medical travel, high-unemployment or disaster counties).</p>
      <p><strong>Cost.</strong> CBO (via <a href="https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/R48755" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CRS R48755</a>) estimates the provision cuts federal Medicaid spending by $325.6 billion over FY2025&ndash;2034 and leaves an average of 4.5 million more people uninsured per year &mdash; overwhelmingly through paperwork, not actual ineligibility.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="fulfill-h">
    <h3 id="fulfill-h">Exactly how you can fulfill the work requirement with $0 earned income</h3>
    <div class="panel accent">
      <ol>
        <li><strong>The income standard (no hours needed).</strong> Monthly income of at least $580 <em>from any source</em> (before taxes) satisfies the rule for that month &mdash; DHCS's official member outreach letter says &ldquo;income from any source,&rdquo; which covers rental income, interest, and trading gains. Seasonal/variable income can average $580/month over six months. <em>Whose income and how it is measured has a flagged nuance &mdash; see <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a>.</em></li>
        <li><strong>Unpaid community service.</strong> 80 hours/month of volunteering or community service counts on its own &mdash; no earnings required. CMS's rule summary and DHCS's member materials both list it. (CMS describes community service conditions such as unpaid work through a structured program of a public or nonprofit organization that tracks hours.)</li>
        <li><strong>School.</strong> Enrollment in an educational program at least half-time satisfies the rule by itself.</li>
        <li><strong>A work program.</strong> 80 hours/month in a job-training program counts.</li>
        <li><strong>Any combination</strong> of work, community service, work program, and education totaling 80 hours/month.</li>
        <li><strong>Self-employment counts as work</strong> per official state Medicaid implementation materials (e.g., <a href="https://ldh.la.gov/medicaid/work-requirements" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Louisiana LDH</a> lists self-employment, unpaid work, and internships) &mdash; but whether hours personally spent trading on Kalshi qualify is <em>not addressed</em> in official member-facing materials and is flagged for review. Do not rely on trading hours without confirming with your county.</li>
        <li><strong>Exemptions.</strong> If any exemption applies to your situation (caregiver of a child 13 or younger, medical condition, etc.), no activity is required at all.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ca-h">
    <h3 id="ca-h">What California is doing for people with $0 earned income</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No work rule until 2027.</strong> Medi-Cal eligibility through the end of 2026 is unchanged: income-based, no asset test for MAGI groups, no work test.</li>
      <li><strong>AB 2161 safeguards</strong> (signed September 14, 2026): data-first verification, continued coverage during the 30-day response period, screening for every other eligibility basis before termination, and resolution of conflicting data before adverse action.</li>
      <li><strong>State subsidy expansion:</strong> $300 million appropriated for 2027 (up from $190 million), extending state premium help to households up to 200% FPL &mdash; one in four Covered California enrollees.</li>
      <li><strong>Outreach:</strong> DHCS texted members from February 2026 and began mailing plain-language notices in late August 2026; counties must include them with applications from October 1 through December 31, 2026.</li>
      <li><strong>Projections:</strong> DHCS estimates up to 233,000 members could lose coverage by June 2027 and about 1.4 million by June 2028 as the rules phase in &mdash; mostly from documentation failures &mdash; which is why responding to county letters quickly matters.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="action-h">
    <h3 id="action-h">Action checklist</h3>
    <div class="panel">
      <ol>
        <li>Estimate this year's MAGI: net rental income + interest + net trading gains (minus allowed capital losses) for the whole tax household. Use the <a href="https://www.healthcare.gov/income-calculator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">healthcare.gov income calculator</a>.</li>
        <li>If MAGI &le; $22,025 (one person): <a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/medi-cal/apply/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">apply for Medi-Cal</a> now &mdash; online (BenefitsCal or Covered California), by mail, phone, or in person. Keep your contact information current with the county.</li>
        <li>Open and answer every Medi-Cal/county letter immediately; from March 2027 renewals come every six months for expansion adults.</li>
        <li>Before January 2027, figure out which compliance path you'll use ($580 any-source income, volunteering, school, training) and keep proof (P&amp;L statements, bank/interest statements, volunteer-hour logs, enrollment records).</li>
        <li>If MAGI is 138&ndash;400% FPL: shop at <a href="https://www.coveredca.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Covered California</a> during open enrollment (November 1, 2026 &ndash; January 31, 2027); report income changes during the year to avoid repaying excess advance credits (caps were removed for tax years after 2025).</li>
        <li>For the Kalshi tax questions (section 1256 vs. other treatment), get a tax professional &mdash; see <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a>.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="unknowns-h">
    <h3 id="unknowns-h">Assumptions, unknowns, and flags</h3>
    <div class="panel warn">
      <p>This research states rules; it does not determine your eligibility. Age, household size, county, actual MAGI, and tax filings are unknown and were not assumed. Values that could not be confirmed from an official source are left blank and flagged, and several inconsistencies between official pages are documented. See the <a href="flagged-for-review.html">Flagged for Review</a> page for the complete list.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
